subproblem.statement:
Let n and l be natural numbers, mu a measure on R^(n+l), w : R^(n+l) -> R, y in R^(n+l), phi : R -> R, and R, eps, s, r real. Assume:
- eps > 0 and mu(B_R(y)) < infinity (closed ball);
- w is a.e.-strongly measurable for mu restricted to the open ball ball y R, and 0 <= w(x) <= 1 for mu-a.e. x in ball y R;
- phi is C^1 and nonincreasing, with phi(u) = 1 for u <= 1 and phi(u) = 0 for u >= 1 + eps;
- 0 < s <= r and (1 + eps) r < R.
Write r_x := |x - y|, phi' := deriv phi and Q(t) := -(integral (r_x/t) phi'(r_x/t) w(x) dmu)/t. Then the lower integral over closedBall y r minus ball y ((1+eps) s) of ofReal(w(x)/r_x^n) dmu is at most ofReal(integral_s^r Q(t)/t^n dt).

Let n and l be natural numbers, mu a measure on R^(n+l), y in R^(n+l), and f : R^(n+l) -> R integrable on the closed ball B_S(y). Let phi : R -> R be C^1 with phi(u) = 0 for all u >= T, where T > 0, and let t > 0 with T t < S. Write r_x := |x - y| and phi' := deriv phi; all integrals are Bochner integrals over R^(n+l). Then:
(a) s -> integral phi(r_x/s) f(x) dmu is continuous at t;
(b) s -> integral (r_x/s) phi'(r_x/s) f(x) dmu is continuous at t;
(c) s -> integral phi(r_x/s) f(x) dmu has derivative -(integral (r_x/t) phi'(r_x/t) f(x) dmu)/t at t.

Let n be a natural number, I, I', E, Q, a : R -> R, and R, M, beta real with beta < 1. Assume, for every t in (0, R):
- I has derivative I'(t) at t;
- E and Q are continuous at t;
- I(t) >= 0;
- n I(t) - t I'(t) + t Q(t) = -E(t);
- |a(t)| <= M t^(-beta) (real power).
Assume also a(t) = E(t)/t^(n+1)/(1 + I(t)/t^n) for every real t. Then for all 0 < s <= r < R, with interval integrals,
exp(-integral_0^r a)(1 + I(r)/r^n) - exp(-integral_0^s a)(1 + I(s)/s^n) = integral_s^r exp(-integral_0^t a) (Q(t)/t^n) dt.

Let n >= 1 and l be natural numbers and p > n real. Let mu be a measure on R^(n+l), H : R^(n+l) -> R^(n+l), w : R^(n+l) -> R, y in R^(n+l), R > 0, Lambda > 0, 0 < eps <= 1, phi : R -> R, and I, E, a : R -> R. Write r_x := |x - y|, phi' := deriv phi and kappa := (p - n)/(4p). Assume:
- mu(B_R(y)) < infinity (closed ball), and H is mu-integrable on B_R(y);
- (ofReal(R^(p-n)) * lower integral over B_R(y) of |H|^p dmu)^(1/p) <= ofReal(kappa Lambda);
- w is a.e.-strongly measurable for mu restricted to ball y R, and 0 <= w(x) <= 1 for mu-a.e. x in ball y R;
- phi is C^1 and nonincreasing, with phi(u) = 1 for u <= 1 and phi(u) = 0 for u >= 1 + eps;
- radial identity: for every rho > 0 with (1+eps) rho < R, integral (n phi(r_x/rho) + (r_x/rho) phi'(r_x/rho)(1 - w(x))) dmu = - integral phi(r_x/rho)(x - y).H(x) dmu;
- for all real t: I(t) = integral phi(r_x/t) dmu, E(t) = integral phi(r_x/t)(x - y).H(x) dmu, and a(t) = E(t)/t^(n+1)/(1 + I(t)/t^n).
Put Phi~(t) := exp(-integral_0^t a)(1 + I(t)/t^n). Then:
(1) for every t > 0 with (1+eps) t < R, a is continuous at t and |a(t)| <= 2 kappa Lambda R^(-1) (t/R)^(-n/p);
(2) for all 0 < s <= r with (1+eps) r < R: Phi~(s) <= Phi~(r), and the lower integral over closedBall y r minus ball y ((1+eps) s) of ofReal(w(x)/|x - y|^n) dmu is at most ofReal(exp(Lambda/2)(Phi~(r) - Phi~(s))).

Let n >= 1 and l be natural numbers and p > n real. Let mu be a measure on R^(n+l), H : R^(n+l) -> R^(n+l), w : R^(n+l) -> R, y in R^(n+l), R > 0 and 0 < Lambda <= 1. Write r_x := |x - y|, phi' := deriv phi for a function phi, and kappa := (p - n)/(4p). Assume:
- mu(B_R(y)) < infinity (closed ball), and H is mu-integrable on B_R(y);
- (ofReal(R^(p-n)) * lower integral over B_R(y) of |H|^p dmu)^(1/p) <= ofReal(kappa Lambda);
- w is a.e.-strongly measurable for mu restricted to ball y R, and 0 <= w(x) <= 1 for mu-a.e. x in ball y R;
- radial identity: for every C^1 function phi : R -> R and every T with phi(t) = 1 for t <= 1 and phi(t) = 0 for t >= T, and every rho > 0 with T rho < R, integral (n phi(r_x/rho) + (r_x/rho) phi'(r_x/rho)(1 - w(x))) dmu = - integral phi(r_x/rho)(x - y).H(x) dmu.
For F : R -> R put Phi(r) := F(r) (r^n)^(-1) mu(B_r(y)).toReal + F(r) - 1, and let alpha' := 1 - n/p. Then there is F : R -> R such that:
(i) for every r in (0, R): exp(-Lambda) <= F(r) <= exp(Lambda) and |F(r) - 1| <= Lambda (r/R)^(alpha');
(ii) for all 0 < s <= r < R: Phi(s) <= Phi(r), and the lower integral over B_r(y) minus B_s(y) of ofReal(w(x)/|x - y|^n) dmu is at most ofReal(exp(Lambda)(Phi(r) - Phi(s))).
